    <detaileddescription>
<para>Hamming Erasure Coder</para>
<para>Implements a generalised RAID-2 / Hamming-code erasure coder operating at shard (block) granularity rather than at the bit level.</para>
<para>Parity assignment: Parity shard p (0-indexed) covers every data shard j (0-indexed) for which bit p is set in the 1-based position (j + 1):</para>
<para>parity[p] = XOR{ data[j] for all j in [0, data_shards) where ((j + 1) &gt;&gt; p) &amp; 1 == 1 }</para>
<para>Properties:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Encode / decode uses pure XOR — no Galois-Field arithmetic needed.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Single data-shard failure can always be recovered via syndrome detection (O(data_shards) work per byte).</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>A missing parity shard can be recomputed directly from data shards.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Best configured with parity_shards = ceil(log2(data_shards + r + 1)) so that syndromes are unique for every possible single failure.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>When more than one data shard is missing the coder attempts iterative repair using parity shards that cover exactly one of the missing shards; if impossible it throws std::runtime_error.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>Example: 4 data shards → 3 parity shards (Hamming(7,4) shard analogue) </para>
    </detaileddescription>
